WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:00.011 --> 00:00:06.191
The universe is magnificent and vast. Hundreds&nbsp;
of billions of galaxies, trillions of stars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.191 --> 00:00:12.671
and even more planets. If even the tiniest&nbsp;
fraction are habitable, then the Universe should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.671 --> 00:00:19.151
be teeming with life. And yet we see nothing,&nbsp;
only vast emptiness. Where is everyone else?

00:00:20.231 --> 00:00:27.791
The answer to this riddle could be as exciting&nbsp;
as it is creepy: we are early, born before almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.791 --> 00:00:35.771
all other life – but very soon this may change.&nbsp;
Not only might aliens appear, they could quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.771 --> 00:00:41.111
surround us. An irreversible competition&nbsp;
for the universe might be about to begin.

00:00:50.651 --> 00:00:56.051
While this video is based on scientific papers,&nbsp;
we are presenting interesting ideas based on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.051 --> 00:01:00.191
little data and lots of extrapolation,&nbsp;
so take them with a grain of salt.

00:01:00.191 --> 00:01:05.771
Ok! We need to look at three essential questions&nbsp;
to understand the galactic competition:

00:01:07.271 --> 00:01:10.451
How fast can bacteria build spaceships?

00:01:10.451 --> 00:01:12.971
To become a starfaring civilization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.971 --> 00:01:16.931
life as we know it needs to master&nbsp;
a number of very hard steps.

00:01:16.931 --> 00:01:21.251
It starts with dead stuff turning&nbsp;
into the building blocks of life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.971 --> 00:01:27.551
Then it needs to organize into self-contained&nbsp;
cells. Those cells have to learn to work together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.551 --> 00:01:34.631
to form multicellular organisms. This keeps going&nbsp;
until complex creatures with big brains learn to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.631 --> 00:01:40.691
use tools and language. Civilization has to&nbsp;
be formed from cultures that value progress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.691 --> 00:01:46.188
and technological development. And then they need&nbsp;
to actually venture out beyond their home planet.

00:01:46.188 --> 00:01:47.891
We don’t really know how hard these steps&nbsp;
are or how many of them are necessary. On&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.891 --> 00:01:53.231
Earth life appeared basically as soon as the&nbsp;
oceans formed. But then it took two billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.231 --> 00:01:58.571
years to make the step from single cells to&nbsp;
multicellular organisms, and two billion more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.571 --> 00:02:05.111
for us to appear. Culture, civilization and&nbsp;
space travel developed super quickly though.

00:02:05.111 --> 00:02:11.651
Do things always take that long or was&nbsp;
this actually exceptionally fast? Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.651 --> 00:02:18.131
passing one step does not mean the next one is&nbsp;
guaranteed: multicellularity evolved over 25&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.131 --> 00:02:22.871
times independently on Earth, but there’s&nbsp;
only one species that builds spaceships.

00:02:23.531 --> 00:02:28.271
We don’t know how many steps life needs to&nbsp;
pass and how long they take to give rise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.271 --> 00:02:33.011
to a technological civilization but there&nbsp;
are probably many, and it is likely that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.011 --> 00:02:36.731
on trillions of planets, life has&nbsp;
been trying for billions of years.

00:02:37.991 --> 00:02:41.531
Since we don’t see any other&nbsp;
technological civilizations out there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.531 --> 00:02:45.971
it might well be that we are a rare&nbsp;
exception. We might be among the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.971 --> 00:02:50.291
or even THE first technological&nbsp;
civilization in the milky way.

00:02:51.311 --> 00:02:54.431
But this is just one piece of the puzzle – on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.431 --> 00:02:57.251
top of that we may have just&nbsp;
hit the perfect time window.

00:02:57.911 --> 00:03:02.531
Why does humanity exist at&nbsp;
this time in the Universe?

00:03:02.531 --> 00:03:05.651
The universe is already 13.8 billion years old,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.651 --> 00:03:11.471
but it is unlikely that many other technological&nbsp;
civilizations had a chance to appear before us.

00:03:12.251 --> 00:03:16.631
Because in the earlier Universe life would&nbsp;
have had a pretty hard time to emerge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.631 --> 00:03:19.991
let alone thrive, because it&nbsp;
was such a hostile environment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.771 --> 00:03:26.351
Early stars constantly blew up, galaxies&nbsp;
crashed into each other and supermassive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.351 --> 00:03:32.591
black holes vomited massive amounts of radiation.&nbsp;
Enough to sterilize galaxies over and over again.

00:03:33.371 --> 00:03:39.491
Our Sun was born right at the end of this cosmic&nbsp;
death show. The Universe has never been more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.491 --> 00:03:45.491
welcoming to life than it is now. So humanity&nbsp;
has arrived at a very convenient spot in time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.491 --> 00:03:50.411
maybe the earliest reasonably possible&nbsp;
for life to thrive. What about the future?

00:03:51.251 --> 00:03:57.011
The Sun burns brighter than 90% of the stars&nbsp;
in our galaxy and will keep getting brighter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.011 --> 00:04:02.351
In about a billion years, it will boil all of&nbsp;
Earth’s oceans and then become a giant that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.351 --> 00:04:07.631
swallows it whole. So in the galactic&nbsp;
context, the sun is very short-lived.

00:04:07.631 --> 00:04:14.231
Most stars are red dwarfs that can sustain&nbsp;
habitable planets for tens of trillions of years!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.311 --> 00:04:22.271
Life on these planets has an incredibly long time&nbsp;
window to appear and pass the hard steps. Even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.271 --> 00:04:27.311
knowing nothing about how rare or common life is,&nbsp;
this makes it way more likely for technological&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.311 --> 00:04:31.331
civilizations to appear on one of those long-lived&nbsp;
planets some time in the future than in the past.

00:04:32.171 --> 00:04:36.191
Because, if civilizations appear at&nbsp;
random in the Milky Way within a time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.191 --> 00:04:41.231
window of a trillion years, then very&nbsp;
few, if any, would appear before today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.251 --> 00:04:46.091
Then a couple more arrive in this period&nbsp;
of a billion years that we are in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.091 --> 00:04:51.251
before all starfaring civilizations that&nbsp;
could ever exist emerge all together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.911 --> 00:04:58.511
This weird tsunami-like distribution is the result&nbsp;
of both the hard steps model and something else:

00:04:59.111 --> 00:05:04.631
A sort of deadline for any spacefaring&nbsp;
civilization. Any civilization coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.631 --> 00:05:11.171
after will find it hard to have room to survive,&nbsp;
so all potential life has to cram in before it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.891 --> 00:05:17.051
Humanity exists now because otherwise&nbsp;
we might have missed this deadline.

00:05:17.831 --> 00:05:21.011
What or who creates this deadline?

00:05:21.971 --> 00:05:24.371
Why aren’t aliens already on Earth?

00:05:25.991 --> 00:05:32.411
Humans are curious, expansionist and hungry for&nbsp;
energy. We have spread over the world and made it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.411 --> 00:05:38.891
our own. Our technology has been improving over&nbsp;
time, first slowly, then breathtakingly fast.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.611 --> 00:05:44.111
If these things do not change drastically,&nbsp;
and our descendants want to prosper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.111 --> 00:05:51.191
they will expand into space. We could construct&nbsp;
a dyson swarm for endless energy and transform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.191 --> 00:05:56.471
planets into new homes. We could cross&nbsp;
interstellar distances, allowing us to reach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.471 --> 00:06:03.071
for planets around distant stars. If we have the&nbsp;
motivation we can become a galactic civilization.

00:06:03.731 --> 00:06:09.131
A civilization that does this sort of stuff can&nbsp;
be called “loud”, because its activity creates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.131 --> 00:06:16.331
“noise”. Signs that can be detected from far away.&nbsp;
Imagine someone in a forest, cutting down trees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.331 --> 00:06:22.751
starting fires and laying down roads. The more&nbsp;
intense their work, the easier they are to notice.

00:06:22.751 --> 00:06:26.171
An expanding technological&nbsp;
civilization would probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.171 --> 00:06:30.191
be hard to miss. Our telescopes&nbsp;
would pick up all that energy and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.191 --> 00:06:34.331
we would clearly identify artificial&nbsp;
interference with stars and planets.

00:06:34.991 --> 00:06:39.311
Another consequence of this business is that&nbsp;
it is very disruptive to the environment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.971 --> 00:06:46.151
Clearing a forest means the end of its wildlife.&nbsp;
Human activity has left no chance for a squirrel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.151 --> 00:06:51.431
civilization to appear. Not because we hated&nbsp;
squirrels, it’s simply that the thought that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.431 --> 00:06:55.571
they might want to do that at some point&nbsp;
never crossed our minds and we needed wood.

00:06:56.351 --> 00:07:00.371
Similarly, if Loud civilizations were&nbsp;
running around the galaxy in the past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.371 --> 00:07:05.831
terraforming planets or harvesting the energy&nbsp;
of stars, they may have prevented our existence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.491 --> 00:07:10.391
Had aliens started colonising earth&nbsp;
while we were still sludge in the oceans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.391 --> 00:07:16.691
that sludge would never have turned into&nbsp;
humans. This is how loud aliens create a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.691 --> 00:07:21.311
deadline for new civilizations appearing.&nbsp;
The galaxy may have trillions of years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.311 --> 00:07:25.811
to create life, but there may only be a&nbsp;
short window for it to spread and thrive.

00:07:26.471 --> 00:07:32.051
Even if a loud civilization respects planets with&nbsp;
naturally-occuring life and expands around them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.051 --> 00:07:36.551
like humans do with wildlife reserves&nbsp;
– any civilization on such a planet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.551 --> 00:07:41.831
would not be able to expand, ever.&nbsp;
Trapped forever on a tiny island.

00:07:42.671 --> 00:07:46.631
But here we are, so Loud aliens&nbsp;
were probably never here.

00:07:47.291 --> 00:07:54.011
What about aliens that don’t expand? They would&nbsp;
be ‘Quiet’ aliens. They’re probably limited to one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.011 --> 00:07:59.711
star system and don’t have a noticeable impact&nbsp;
on their cosmic surroundings. Humanity is like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.711 --> 00:08:04.271
this right now. We wouldn’t be able to detect&nbsp;
ourselves from the other side of the Milky Way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.931 --> 00:08:08.531
If they stay quiet forever, maybe&nbsp;
because of their culture or abilities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.531 --> 00:08:11.111
then they are not really a concern for us.

00:08:11.831 --> 00:08:18.431
We only have one sample to draw from: Humanity,&nbsp;
and right now we are on the path to becoming loud.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.511 --> 00:08:23.651
If we are not special, and succeed&nbsp;
anyway, then any other civilization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.651 --> 00:08:28.631
with the motivation and resources to would&nbsp;
eventually expand beyond its planet of origin.
 &nbsp;

00:08:29.231 --> 00:08:33.011
Ok, what is the consequence of&nbsp;
all these assumptions and ideas?

00:08:34.391 --> 00:08:37.631
Grave consequences: Race to the Stars

00:08:38.531 --> 00:08:44.591
If we are really early, then eventually, others&nbsp;
will catch up with us. Civilizations will emerge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.591 --> 00:08:49.991
all over the place. And these new aliens will&nbsp;
look at space, see no signs of life and come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.991 --> 00:08:55.151
to the same conclusion: they exist because Loud&nbsp;
civilizations have not yet taken over everything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.151 --> 00:09:00.071
but it only takes one Loud civilization&nbsp;
to crowd them out of the entire galaxy.

00:09:01.151 --> 00:09:07.991
They, like us, will face an important decision:&nbsp;
do they stay Quiet, take it easy and tend to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.991 --> 00:09:12.791
their planet for as long as possible, or do they&nbsp;
start expanding to take a chunk of the galaxy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.791 --> 00:09:20.291
before someone else arrives? Meeting others&nbsp;
does not necessarily mean war or conflict. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.291 --> 00:09:25.631
it means that new borders will arise, limits&nbsp;
that may persist forever. In the worst case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.631 --> 00:09:29.711
a civilization could be completely&nbsp;
enveloped by the empires of others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.311 --> 00:09:34.931
eternally doomed to be a galactic&nbsp;
backwater, without control over their fate.

00:09:35.651 --> 00:09:38.591
So if we want a seat at&nbsp;
the galactic adults’ table,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.591 --> 00:09:46.271
we best get to work. If we really are early, we&nbsp;
have an incredible opportunity. To mold thousands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.271 --> 00:09:52.211
or even millions of planets according&nbsp;
to our vision and dreams. And one day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.211 --> 00:09:57.971
when we meet others, we can greet them and&nbsp;
meet them as equals. Wouldn’t that be nice?

